WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:20.731
Hello. My name is Mohammad Ali Jaffry. I am an architect. With ten years of architectural experience. Welcome, to this episode. The history of microservices.

2
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.564
Nobody invented microservices on a given day.

3
00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:52.349
The word was argued over in 2011 and 2012, and fixed in public on the 25th of March 2014, when James Lewis and Martin Fowler published an article called, simply, ‘Microservices’.

4
00:00:52.729 --> 00:00:57.454
But the practice was running for more than a decade before the industry had a stable name for it.

5
00:00:58.434 --> 00:00:59.665
The roots are old.

6
00:01:00.705 --> 00:01:07.137
Fowler and Lewis pointed at Unix: small programs, each doing one job, joined by simple pipes.

7
00:01:07.517 --> 00:01:12.799
Lewis’s 2012 talk was titled ‘Microservices — Java, the Unix Way’.

8
00:01:13.499 --> 00:01:16.611
The organizational idea is older still.

9
00:01:17.651 --> 00:01:24.849
In 1968 Melvin Conway wrote that a system copies the communication structure of the organization that designs it.

10
00:01:25.229 --> 00:01:30.094
That sentence later justified drawing service boundaries along business teams.

11
00:01:30.794 --> 00:01:40.291
And in April 1996, two Gartner analysts described a ‘service oriented’ architecture: shared logic and data, reached through interfaces.

12
00:01:41.271 --> 00:01:53.833
Through the 2000s that idea arrived in enterprises as SOAP, central governance, and an enterprise service bus — a central piece of middleware that routed, transformed, and applied rules.

13
00:01:54.213 --> 00:02:04.592
Lewis and Fowler wrote their article because ‘SOA’ had come to mean too many incompatible things, and the systems they admired were a sharper variant: smart services, dull pipes.

14
00:02:05.572 --> 00:02:08.335
Amazon was already doing it.

15
00:02:09.375 --> 00:02:18.593
In 1998 senior engineers wrote the Distributed Computing Manifesto — Werner Vogels published it in 2022.

16
00:02:18.973 --> 00:02:25.080
In a 2006 interview Vogels said the two-tier monolith had stopped scaling around 2001.

17
00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:33.570
The rule they adopted: encapsulate the data with the logic that uses it, allow no outside database access, and share no data between services.

18
00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:45.084
There is also the famous Bezos mandate of around 2002 — but that comes from Steve Yegge’s 2011 retelling, so the chapter marks it as secondary evidence.

19
00:02:46.124 --> 00:02:54.739
The firmer fact is public: Amazon Web Services launched in 2006 by externalizing infrastructure Amazon had built for itself.

20
00:02:55.719 --> 00:02:58.958
Netflix made the style famous at web scale.

21
00:02:59.998 --> 00:03:08.555
In August 2008 a data-center outage took Netflix down for days, just as streaming load was arriving on top of the DVD business.

22
00:03:08.935 --> 00:03:15.796
The decision was to leave the data center and rebuild for the cloud — Adrian Cockcroft called the result ‘fine-grained SOA’.

23
00:03:16.496 --> 00:03:27.503
The tools that leaked out — Eureka, Hystrix, Zuul, and Chaos Monkey — are the public form of one 2014 characteristic: design for failure, because a remote call will fail.

24
00:03:28.483 --> 00:03:29.458
Then the name.

25
00:03:30.498 --> 00:03:37.568
In May 2011 a workshop of architects near Venice found they had been building the same style under different labels.

26
00:03:37.948 --> 00:03:44.229
Lewis presented it in Kraków in March 2012; Fred George was speaking about the same ideas.

27
00:03:44.929 --> 00:03:49.980
In May 2012 the group chose ‘microservices’.

28
00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.471
And then the platform caught up.

29
00:03:54.511 --> 00:04:02.928
Docker was open-sourced on the 20th of March 2013, which made a service a portable, deployable unit.

30
00:04:03.308 --> 00:04:10.901
Kubernetes’s first commit is the 6th of June 2014; Eric Brewer announced it at DockerCon four days later.

31
00:04:11.601 --> 00:04:19.484
Kubernetes 1.0 and the Cloud Native Computing Foundation were announced together on the 21st of July 2015.

32
00:04:19.864 --> 00:04:23.916
Sam Newman’s ‘Building Microservices’ became the practice manual that year.

33
00:04:24.296 --> 00:04:36.835
Linkerd in 2016 and Istio in 2017 added a traffic layer — discovery, retries, routing — because operating hundreds of services had become its own problem.

34
00:04:37.815 --> 00:04:40.160
Here is the part people forget.

35
00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:53.054
On the 13th of May 2015, in the same year as the boom, Fowler published ‘MicroservicePremium’: distribution adds complexity and risk, and teams that take it on before they need it get into trouble.

36
00:04:53.434 --> 00:05:01.154
From 2015 to about 2021 the correction was monolith-first advice and the modular monolith.

37
00:05:01.854 --> 00:05:09.737
Many systems called microservices were one deployment unit sliced across the network, still sharing a database and a release train.

38
00:05:10.717 --> 00:05:14.119
So what did the 2014 article fix?

39
00:05:15.159 --> 00:05:18.642
Lewis and Fowler refused a formal definition.

40
00:05:19.682 --> 00:05:22.631
They listed characteristics they kept seeing.

41
00:05:23.671 --> 00:05:25.900
Services as components.

42
00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:29.901
Organized around business capabilities.

43
00:05:30.941 --> 00:05:33.820
Products, not projects — you build it, you run it.

44
00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:37.902
Smart endpoints and dumb pipes.

45
00:05:38.942 --> 00:05:41.090
Decentralized governance.

46
00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:44.057
Decentralized data.

47
00:05:45.097 --> 00:05:47.407
Infrastructure automation.

48
00:05:48.447 --> 00:05:50.061
Design for failure.

49
00:05:51.101 --> 00:05:52.912
Evolutionary design.

50
00:05:53.952 --> 00:05:55.810
Nine characteristics.

51
00:05:57.130 --> 00:06:06.000
The style was a rejection of one central brain — the enterprise service bus — in favor of many smart services and dull pipes.

52
00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:16.283
And that is the baseline for everything that follows: any claim that AI changed the architecture has to say which of these nine characteristics actually moved.

53
00:06:17.603 --> 00:06:20.181
Three things to carry away.

54
00:06:21.221 --> 00:06:21.871
One.

55
00:06:22.911 --> 00:06:30.016
The practice is decades older than the name: the name from 2012, the description from 25 March 2014.

56
00:06:30.716 --> 00:06:31.215
Two.

57
00:06:32.255 --> 00:06:38.676
Amazon and Netflix got there by necessity — a monolith that stopped scaling, an outage that lasted days.

58
00:06:39.056 --> 00:06:39.799
Three.

59
00:06:40.839 --> 00:06:46.400
The nine characteristics are the ruler; the 2015 ‘premium’ is the warning that travels with it.

60
00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:52.720
Next: the pattern language — sixteen questions every microservice architect answers, in order.
